-- Pilot: a NEW mm0/mm1 file that reuses set.mm as a *library* instead of
-- restating Lean terms as axioms.  Every proof below is a citation of a
-- set.mm theorem label: `mm0-rs` elaborates the citation against
-- metamath/set.mm0, and `mm0-c` re-checks the .mmb without knowing where the
-- proof came from.
--
-- Provisioning: mm0/metamath/{set.mm0,set.mmu} produced by `mm0-from-mm`
-- (docs/lean4mm0.md §11).  Because set.mm0 is converter output, labels are
-- spelled as the converter spells them: `0lt1` -> `_0lt1`, `.` -> `_`.
--
-- Two things this pilot establishes by construction:
--   * `delimiter` declarations are NOT inherited through `import`, so a file
--     that writes parentheses in formulas must redeclare them (below);
--   * a citation names only the *hypothesis* proofs — substitutions are
--     inferred, so `'(ltlei _0re _1re)`, not `'(ltlei cc0 c1 _0re _1re)`.
--
-- Check:
--   mm0-rs compile pilot.mm1 pilot.mmb
--   cat metamath/set.mm0  <the 5 statements, as .mm0>  > pilot_joined.mm0
--   mm0-c pilot.mmb < pilot_joined.mm0      # rc=0; alter any statement -> 255

import "metamath/set.mmu";

delimiter $ ( $ $ ) $;

-- 1. Plain citation: set.mm's own proof, imported and re-checked.
pub theorem pilot_zero_lt_one: $ wbr cc0 c1 clt $ = '(_0lt1);

pub theorem pilot_one_re: $ wcel c1 cr $ = '(_1re);

pub theorem pilot_zero_ne_one: $ wne cc0 c1 $ = '(_0ne1);

-- 2. Instantiation: set.mm's `id` at a variable this file introduces.
pub theorem pilot_id (ph: wff): $ wi ph ph $ = '(id);

-- 3. Composition through two citations: 0 e. RR and 1 e. RR discharge
--    `ltlei`'s hypotheses, yielding "0 < 1 implies 0 <= 1".
pub theorem pilot_zero_le_one:
  $ wi (wbr cc0 c1 clt) (wbr cc0 c1 cle) $ = '(ltlei _0re _1re);
